WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:06.410
Superhero movies are a major movie staple,
but they weren’t always popular.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:13.110
Throughout the 1970s, '80s, and '90s, movie
and television producers adapted comics titles

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.339
with varying degrees of success.

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:20.720
For every Superman or Batman, there were a
half a dozen heroes who flopped.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.509
Here are a few superhero movies most people
have completely forgotten about.

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:27.509
The Shadow

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:34.510
This this 1994 Alec Baldwin vehicle adapted
the 1930s pulp hero to the big screen.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:40.270
Lamont Cranston is a wealthy playboy who,
as the Shadow, uses a handful of mystical

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.640
powers, such as mind control, to fight crime.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:49.710
Universal Studios poured a lot of money into
what it wanted to be a big franchise...but

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:55.370
they didn’t spend quite enough on the story,
about a descendant of Genghis Khan determined

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.690
to detonate an atomic bomb.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.250
There ultimately just wasn’t a huge built-in
audience for a property that was popular before

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.080
most of its potential consumers were even
alive.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.530
The Phantom

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:15.320
Based on the classic comic strip, 1996's The
Phantom stars Billy Zane as Kit Walker, another

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.910
wealthy playboy who secretly fights crime
as a masked hero.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:23.960
But let’s be honest: it's hard to take a
hero seriously while he's running around in

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.720
a goofy purple jumpsuit.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.710
If you're daring enough to watch The Phantom,
look for an early performance from Catherine

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.040
Zeta-Jones as the villain's female sidekick.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.700
“Get a good look.”

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Fantastic Four

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.580
“It’s clobbering time”

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:49.260
Ten years before 20th Century Fox made its
first Fantastic Four movie, legendary B-movie

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:54.440
producer Roger Corman developed his own super-low-budget
adaptation.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.690
Few people saw it because it was never officially
released in theaters or on video.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:04.900
But bootleg copies have been widely available
at comic conventions and online.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.629
It was only ever made in the first place so
that the studio could retain the rights to

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.010
the characters before they expired.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.579
Doctor Strange

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:20.969
Years before making his 2016 big-screen debut,
Marvel's Doctor Strange starred in a 1978

00:02:20.969 --> 00:02:22.409
made-for-TV movie.

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:28.219
It tells the origin story of Strange, played
by journeyman actor Peter Hooten, who transforms

00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:31.769
from being a medical doctor into a sorcerer.

00:02:31.769 --> 00:02:36.999
The effects are pretty dated, and the costumes
look like they came out of a circus.

00:02:36.999 --> 00:02:43.041
One notable thing about the 1978 Strange:
a young, foxy Jessica Walter portrays the

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:45.180
villain, Morgan Le Fay.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.450
But these days, you're probably more likely
to recognize her as Lucille Bluth from Arrested

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.500
Development and the voice of Malory from Archer.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.069
The Punisher

00:02:56.069 --> 00:03:02.200
If you need to watch Dolph Lundgren murder
dozens of Yakuza, then the 1989 adaptation

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:06.140
of Marvel’s The Punisher is right up your
alley.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:11.309
Lundgren plays Frank Castle, a grieving cop
who avenges his family's death.

00:03:11.309 --> 00:03:17.250
The greatest fight in the film, however, may
be Lundgren's struggle with the English language.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.159
“I don't need your help."

00:03:19.159 --> 00:03:20.159
Darkman

00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:25.779
Liam Neeson portrays scientist Peyton Westlake,
whose life is forever altered after he's attacked

00:03:25.779 --> 00:03:29.529
by ruthless mobsters and left horribly scarred.

00:03:29.529 --> 00:03:34.799
In a convenient twist, he’s also now super
strong and resistant to pain, allowing him

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:37.090
to pay back those who've wronged him.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:43.919
Sam Raimi, director of the Evil Dead and original
Spider-Man movies, co-wrote and directed Darkman.

00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:49.360
It's got many hallmarks of his work, including
slapstick humor, over-the-top action, and

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.260
of course, a cameo from Bruce Campbell.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.269
A bunch of Captain America movies

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:58.790
In 2011, Captain America: The First Avenger,
was a hit.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:04.959
But The First Avenger wasn't actually the
first Captain America movie—not by a long

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:05.959
shot.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:11.349
Filmed in 1990, and released on video two
years later, Captain America starred Matt

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:16.359
Salinger, son of the legendarily reclusive
author J.D. Salinger.

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:21.859
It tells the now familiar tale of would-be
soldier Steve Rogers, who becomes a superhero

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:25.800
and fights his Nazi nemesis: the Red Skull.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:32.930
And back in 1979, CBS aired two made-for-TV
movies, appropriately titled Captain America

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:34.590
and Captain America 2.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:38.759
Both starred B-movie action king Reb Brown
as Cap.

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:44.889
The daredevil stunts Evel Knievel was popular
at the time, so Captain America spends most

00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:47.940
of his time on a motorcycle.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.000
Condorman

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:55.060
In this 1981 Disney movie, a guy named Woody
Wilkins is a comic book artist who decides

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.840
to become a real-world version of his creation.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.919
He comes to the attention of the CIA, and
the agency recruits Wilkins for a mission

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:09.680
to save a beautiful KGB agent who plans to
defect to the United States.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.479
And apparently in a bid to fully waste taxpayer's
money, they build Wilkins his own Condorman-themed

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:16.600
car and boat.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.030
You know, for a guy who can fly.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:22.310
Various ‘70s Spider-Man movies

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:29.720
Spider-Man was the star of a made-for-TV movie
in the late 1970s, called, appropriately Spider-Man.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:35.159
The 90-minute film served as the pilot to
the CBS show The Amazing Spider-Man.

00:05:35.159 --> 00:05:40.460
Episodes from the show's two-season run were
eventually repackaged as standalone movies

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:46.340
and sold on video around the world as Spider-Man
Strikes Back and Spider-Man: The Dragon's

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.340
Challenge.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.389
Though the show must've been pretty cool for
comics fans at the time, let's just say it

00:05:51.389 --> 00:05:53.669
hasn't aged very well.

00:05:53.669 --> 00:05:56.229
Swamp Thing / The Return of Swamp Thing

00:05:56.229 --> 00:06:01.849
A scientist is transformed into Swamp Thing
after soldiers working for the evil Anton

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:06.069
Arcane attack his laboratory in the Louisiana
bayou.

00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:11.509
The results are more corny than horrific,
with some pretty cheap special effects—even

00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:12.509
for 1982.

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:18.500
And yet, a sequel called The Return of Swamp
Thing was produced in 1989.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:24.639
This time the scientist turned walking lily
pad must once again defeat his nemesis, Anton

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:30.190
Arcane, who somehow survived being horrifically
mutated in the first movie.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:35.290
The only positive thing to say about The Return
of Swamp Thing is that the costume looks a

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:36.290
little better.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:42.330
Well, that, and the super-hot romance between
Swamp Thing and a young Heather Locklear.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.060
Thanks for watching!

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.939
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:57.939 --> 00:07:02.319
And leave us a comment to let us know which
of these movies you remembered after all...

